  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Solve the following for $\\theta\\in[0^\\circ,360^\\circ]$.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p19-q7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$(2\\sin\\theta-1)(\\sin\\theta+1)=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\theta=30^\\circ,150^\\circ,270^\\circ$.",
          "worked": "By the zero-product rule,\n$$2\\sin\\theta-1=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\\sin\\theta+1=0.$$\nThus $\\sin\\theta=\\frac12$ or $\\sin\\theta=-1$. The first gives $30^\\circ,150^\\circ$ and the second gives $270^\\circ$.\n$$\\theta=30^\\circ,150^\\circ,270^\\circ.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$2\\sin^2\\theta-3\\sin\\theta+1=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\theta=30^\\circ,90^\\circ,150^\\circ$.",
          "worked": "$$(2\\sin\\theta-1)(\\sin\\theta-1)=0.$$\nBy the zero-product rule, $\\sin\\theta=\\frac12$ or $\\sin\\theta=1$. The first gives $30^\\circ,150^\\circ$ and the second gives $90^\\circ$.\n$$\\theta=30^\\circ,90^\\circ,150^\\circ.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q7-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$2\\cos^2\\theta-\\cos\\theta-3=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\theta=180^\\circ$.\n\nReject $\\cos\\theta=\\frac32$, since cosine lies in $[-1,1]$.",
          "worked": "$$(2\\cos\\theta-3)(\\cos\\theta+1)=0.$$\nBy the zero-product rule, $\\cos\\theta=\\frac32$ or $\\cos\\theta=-1$. Reject $\\frac32$, since $-1\\leq\\cos\\theta\\leq1$. Hence\n$$\\theta=180^\\circ.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q7-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$\\sec^2\\theta+\\sec\\theta-2=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\theta=0^\\circ,120^\\circ,240^\\circ,360^\\circ$.\n\nFactor in $\\sec\\theta$, then convert each factor solution to cosine.",
          "worked": "$$(\\sec\\theta+2)(\\sec\\theta-1)=0.$$\nBy the zero-product rule, $\\sec\\theta=-2$ or $\\sec\\theta=1$. Thus $\\cos\\theta=-\\frac12$ or $\\cos\\theta=1$. These give $120^\\circ,240^\\circ$ and $0^\\circ,360^\\circ$ respectively. Both endpoints belong to the domain.\n$$\\theta=0^\\circ,120^\\circ,240^\\circ,360^\\circ.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      }
    ]
  },
